{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed581286445/11/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"33067df9\" lines=\"32-48\">\n<description>\nExpand the sparse dot lattice into a 26x26 drawing with thick bars between adjacent matching dots.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Foreground pixels lie on logical rows and columns separated by background spacer rows/columns.\n- A logical row or column is present when it contains at least one foreground pixel.\n\nRule\n- Expand every background spacer row/column to thickness 2.\n- Expand the logical rows/columns equally so the full output is 26x26.\n- Paint each foreground logical pixel as a solid rectangle of its color.\n- Connect adjacent same-color logical pixels horizontally through the intervening spacer.\n- Connect adjacent same-color logical pixels vertically only when neither endpoint is part of a horizontal same-color run.\n\nKey insight\n- The input is a sampled line drawing: horizontal same-color runs define the bars first, and only unclaimed same-color vertical neighbors are joined.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7c66cb00\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nDock two-tone sprites into color-matched horizontal bars with a color swap.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Top area: small two-tone sprites (outer color + inner color).\n- Below: horizontal bars spanning most of the row, each with a fill color and border color on edges.\n\nRule:\n- Match each sprite to the bar whose fill color equals the sprite's outer color.\n- Place the sprite at its original x-position, aligned to the bar's bottom.\n- Recolor: sprite outer → bar border, sprite inner → bar fill.\n- Erase original sprites from the top area.\n\nKey insight: Color matching is inverted — the sprite's dominant color identifies its target bar, then gets swapped to the bar's accent color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b0039139\" lines=\"29-44\">\n<description>\nRecolor a cropped template and repeat it according to a component-count stencil.\n\nInput structure:\n- Solid blue (1) separator rows or columns split the grid into four sections.\n- Section 1 is a black-background shape template; section 2 is a component-count stencil.\n- Sections 3 and 4 are solid color swatches for output foreground and background.\n\nRule:\n- Crop section 1 to the bbox of its non-black pixels and recolor it with the two swatches.\n- Count 4-connected non-black components in section 2.\n- Repeat the recolored crop that many times, inserting one background-colored row/column between copies.\n\nKey insight: The second section encodes only the repeat count; the output orientation\nis inherited from whether the input separators are columns or rows.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8886d717\" lines=\"43-63\">\n<description>\nRepair markers embedded in the main object and copy the remaining markers one pixel toward the solid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- A mostly background grid contains one solid non-background edge line.\n- Apart from that edge and the background, there is one large main object\n  color and a smaller marker color.\n- Each marker is either embedded in the main object, touching it on at\n  least three 4-neighbor sides, or isolated on the background.\n\nRule:\n- Embedded markers are recolored to the main object color.\n- Isolated background markers remain in place and also paint the adjacent\n  background pixel in the direction of the solid edge line.\n- All other pixels are unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\nThe full edge line is a direction cue: it tells which way the background\nmarkers extend, while markers surrounded by the main object are simply\ndefects to be filled.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1190bc91\" lines=\"36-49\">\n<description>\nComplete a sparse grid by casting occluding diagonal rays from the longest run and filling the remaining diagonal-connected regions from the short bars.\n\nInput structure\n- Black-background grid with one unique longest 4-connected foreground object.\n- The longest object is a horizontal or vertical multicolor run; all other foreground objects are shorter monochrome bars.\n\nRule\n- On a blank canvas, process the longest run from left to right or top to bottom; each pixel emits four 45-degree rays that stop at the first already-painted pixel.\n- Each remaining monochrome bar fills every still-blank pixel reachable from it by diagonal steps.\n\nKey insight\n- Earlier pixels in the long run occlude later diagonal rays, and those ray barriers partition the background into diagonal-connected regions for the short bars.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"758abdf0\" lines=\"54-69\">\n<description>\nAdvance short marker bars away from the full edge bar, turning completed two-pixel markers into edge-color marks at the opposite side.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One non-background color forms a complete one-cell-thick bar on exactly one outer edge.\n- The other foreground color appears only in the first two cells inward from that bar, lane by lane.\n\nRule\n- In each lane perpendicular to the edge bar, a one-pixel marker adjacent to the bar grows one more cell inward.\n- A two-pixel marker in those first two inward cells is cleared back to background.\n- The cleared two-pixel marker is replaced by a two-pixel mark of the edge-bar color at the opposite side of the same lane.\n\nKey insight\n- The full edge bar defines both the orientation and the direction of the local two-step marker cycle.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"16de56c4\" lines=\"9-34\">\n<description>\nFill periodic progressions that shoot from a unique grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.  Sparse non-bg dots of several colors.\n- All repeating colors originate from the same grid edge (top, bottom,\n  left, or right — the one with the most non-bg dots).\n- Each repeating color has exactly 2 dots per shooting-direction line;\n  their gap defines the step.\n- Every non-bg dot lies on a shooting-direction line that contains a\n  repeating color.\n\nRule:\n- Determine the unique shooting edge; left/right → rows, top/bottom → columns.\n- For each shooting-direction line, the repeating color has exactly 2 dots;\n  step = gap between them, offset = first position mod step.\n- A different-colored dot aligned to the same beat (pos % step == offset) is\n  a companion: it absorbs the ray and becomes the fill color, limiting the\n  fill range to the span between repeating and companion dots.\n- An unaligned dot is a bystander and left untouched.\n- Without a companion the repeating color fills the entire line.\n\nKey insight: the shooting edge is identified by counting boundary dots;\nthe gap between the two marker dots is the period; a companion sharing the\nmodular class acts as a wall that stops the ray and dictates the paint color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"65b59efc\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nDecode a stamp map: read templates, markers, and palette from a tiled grid, then render stamps.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid divided into K tile columns (each N wide) separated by 1-col dashed separators.\n- Top band (rows 0..N-1): K template masks, one per tile column.\n- Middle band (rows N+1..2N): marker pixels whose color picks a template.\n- Bottom band (rows 2N+2..): K palette swatches giving the output color per template.\n- Separator rows at rows N and 2N+1 (dashed pattern of sep color).\n\nRule:\n- Each marker's local position (lr, lc) within its tile column determines which N×N output block to stamp.\n- The marker's color identifies which template to use; the palette recolors the template.\n\nKey insight: Marker color encodes template index; local position within tile column encodes stamp block.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f3e14006\" lines=\"21-39\">\n<description>\nBuild the woven rectangle determined by the two marked cells on each crossed bar.\n\nInput structure\nA black-background grid contains one long horizontal bar and one long vertical bar.\nEach bar has a dominant base color and exactly two same-colored marker pixels.\n\nRule\nThe horizontal marker pair gives the output column span.  From the horizontal bar\nrow, walk toward the farther vertical marker; rows and columns alternate every\nother cell.  Even step/even offset cells use the horizontal marker color until\nthe nearer vertical marker is reached, then use the vertical marker color.\nOdd row steps use the horizontal base on marker columns and the original\ncrossing color on spacer columns.  Spacer columns on even row steps use the\nvertical base, except at the switch row where they use the crossing color.\n\nKey insight\nThe original cross cell records which base color wins at base/base lattice\ncrossings; the nearer vertical marker is the color-switch boundary.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"58c02a16\" lines=\"10-26\">\n<description>\nExpand the top-left seed mask into a recursive colored pattern and tile it across the full grid.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- All foreground lies in a top-left rectangle whose last row and last column are a solid secondary color.\n- Removing that secondary-color row and column leaves a seed region containing background plus one primary color.\n\nRule\n- Use the primary-color pixels in the seed as a binary mask.\n- Replace each primary seed cell with a copy of the seed mask in the primary color, and each background seed cell\n  with a copy of the seed mask in the secondary color.\n- Repeat and crop that expanded tile to the original grid shape.\n\nKey insight\nThe solid L-shaped secondary-color border is a legend, not output content; it supplies the color used when the\nseed's background cells are expanded.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e9fc42f2\" lines=\"26-43\">\n<description>\nAssemble the foreground objects by docking equal-colored boundary markers edge-to-edge.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color and several 4-connected multicolor foreground\nobjects.  One foreground color is the body color; the less frequent foreground colors are\nboundary connector markers, and each marker color appears once as a free end or twice as a\nmatched docking pair.\n\nRule\nCrop each foreground object to its bounding box and translate the objects so that the two\noccurrences of every paired connector color touch on their exposed boundary sides.  Preserve\neach object's orientation and colors, then return the tight crop around the assembled\nforeground on the original background color.\n\nKey insight\nThe colored marker pixels are ports: matching colors specify which objects must touch, and\nthe marker's side of its object determines the direction of the translation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ff2825db\" lines=\"18-36\">\n<description>\nSelect the majority interior marker color, then redraw the framed board as that color's frame plus its bounding-box outline.\n\nInput structure:\n- The top row is a copied palette made from adjacent equal color pairs.\n- Below it is a one-cell-thick rectangular frame around a black interior.\n- Non-black, non-frame interior pixels are marker colors; one marker color has\n  the unique highest count.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the palette row unchanged.\n- Pick the uniquely most frequent interior marker color.\n- Clear the lower board to black, recolor its outer frame with the picked color,\n  and draw the picked color's interior bounding-box border.\n\nKey insight:\n- The noisy marker cloud is not preserved as pixels; only the majority marker\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
